        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Haskalah</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val">Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, beginning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>; later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a>, the Pale of Settlement and Congress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>; a certain influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> and the Muslim world</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">arose as a defined worldview in the 1770s in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>; spread through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the 19th century; its last stage ended at the close of the 19th century, after the 1881 pogroms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val">Moses Mendelssohn, philosopher and translator of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> into German; Naphtali Hirz Wessely, poet and author of Words of Peace and Truth; Isaac Euchel, founder of Ha-Meassef; Joseph Perl, satirist and school founder; Isaac Baer Levinsohn, called the Russian Mendelssohn; Abraham Mapu, novelist; Judah Leib Gordon, poet; Aleksander Zederbaum, editor of Ha-Melitz</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">it revived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> as a literary language and helped modernise education and culture among Central and Eastern European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, but its later historians judge its part limited; its adherents were divided, and after the 1881 pogroms many turned towards proto-Zionist groups</span></div>
