    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The maskilim, as the movement's activists were called, sought to revive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> as a language of secular literature, teach general subjects alongside Jewish ones, and bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> into the surrounding societies without ending Jewish distinctness.</li><li>The movement clashed with the traditionalist rabbinic elite, which wished to keep old Jewish values and norms in full, and also with radical assimilationists, whom the maskilim called pseudo-maskilim.</li><li>Historians once treated the Haskalah as the main cause of the changes in Jewish society, but later research narrowed its importance: state schools and new economic opportunities rivalled or overshadowed it, and in many regions it had no effect at all.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Abraham Mapu's Ahavat Zion, the first full-length <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> novel, was published in 1853 after twenty-three years of work.</p></aside>
    </section>

