    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1755</span><span class="rf-text">Moses Mendelssohn begins publishing Qohelet Musar, regarded as the first journal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and the beginning of modern writing in the language</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1778</span><span class="rf-text">Maskilim help to found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> Jewish Free School, the first institution among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi Jews</a> to teach general studies beside a reduced traditional curriculum</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1782</span><span class="rf-text">Hartwig Wessely circulates Divrei Shalom v'Emeth, calling on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to add science and the vernacular language to their children's curriculum</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1783</span><span class="rf-text">Isaac Abraham Euchel launches the quarterly Ha-Meassef in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, whose publishers call themselves Maskilim</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1813</span><span class="rf-text">Joseph Perl opens the first modern Jewish school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> at Tarnopol</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1819</span><span class="rf-text">Joseph Perl publishes Megaleh Tmirin, a satire on Hasidic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1860</span><span class="rf-text">Aleksander Zederbaum launches Ha-Melitz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a>, whose motto makes Haskalah the standard name of the movement</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1881</span><span class="rf-text">Pogroms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a> lead many adherents of the Haskalah to turn towards proto-Zionist groups such as Hibbat Zion</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/voltaire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/voltaire.jpg" alt="Voltaire" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolas_de_Largilli%C3%A8re_-_Portrait_de_Voltaire_(1694-1778)_en_1718_-_P208_-_mus%C3%A9e_Carnavalet_-_5_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nicolas de Largillière, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As long as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived in segregated communities and contact with their gentile neighbours was limited, the rabbi was the most influential member of the Jewish community. Besides being a religious scholar, a rabbi acted as a civil judge in all cases in which both parties were Jews, and sometimes held other administrative powers together with the community elders. The rabbinate was the highest aim of many Jewish boys, and study of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> was the way to it. The rabbinic courts and the board of elders were the traditional community institutions that the maskilim later sought to limit.</p><p>The example of Moses Mendelssohn (1729–86), a Prussian Jew, led the movement. His success as a philosopher and man of letters revealed possibilities of integration and acceptance of Jews among non-Jews. Knowledge of German was necessary to enter cultured German circles, and Mendelssohn provided a means of acquiring it in his German translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>, which became a bridge for ambitious young Jews into the world of secular knowledge. The Biur, a grammatical commentary prepared under his supervision, was designed to counteract traditional rabbinical methods of exegesis.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> was the city of origin. Its middle-class Jews and intellectual elites were exposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> thinkers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Diderot</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>, and the movement is often called the Berlin Haskalah. A further precondition was a cultural change among the Parnassim, the affluent communal wardens, who were open to outside society and had to teach their children secular subjects.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Haskalah arose as a defined ideological worldview in the 1770s, as its proponents engaged with the philosophical and scientific ideas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Age of Enlightenment</a>. The maskilim wished to replace the values of the Ashkenazim of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> with a philosophy that embraced the liberal, rationalistic notions of the 18th and 19th centuries. Even the moderates thought the state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> deplorable in morals, cultural creativity and economic productivity, and held that the base and corrupt elements of Jewish existence should be shed and only the true, positive ones kept.</p><p>Their programme was practical. They urged Jews to learn European culture and the language of their lands, to discard traditional dress, and to be patriotic and loyal to the new centralised governments. They opposed self-segregation and criticised child marriage, the use of anathema to enforce community will, and the concentration on almost only religious studies. They preached agriculture, trades and the liberal professions in place of traditional Jewish occupations.</p><p>Language stood at the centre. The maskilim wanted a refined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> for internal use and the local vernacular for external dealings, and they almost universally abhorred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yiddish">Yiddish</a>, then called Judaeo-German, as a corrupt dialect. On faith they had no uniform agenda: they judged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> by rationalism, revived Jewish philosophy as an alternative to Kabbalah, and sought a literal reading of scripture. Some on the right only wanted a rationalist approach; on the extreme left some ventured towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deism">Deism</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The literary beginnings came first. In 1755 Mendelssohn began publishing Qohelet Musar, the first journal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>. In 1778 supporters of the movement were among the founders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> Jewish Free School, which taught general studies in addition to a reformulated and reduced traditional curriculum. In 1782 Hartwig Wessely, one of the most traditional and moderate maskilim, circulated Divrei Shalom v'Emeth, urging that science and the vernacular enter the curriculum. Historian Shmuel Feiner judged that Wessely, consciously or not, insinuated a challenge to the supremacy of sacred teachings, which mobilised the rabbis against the nascent Haskalah.</p><p>In 1783 Isaac Abraham Euchel launched Ha-Meassef in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, later Berlin; at its height it had 272 subscribers from Shklow in the east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in the west, and it was printed with growing intervals until 1797. Later periodicals included Bikurei ha-I'tim in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> (1820–1831), Kerem Hemed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> (1834–1857), Ha-Karmel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> (1860–1880), Ha-Tsefirah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and Ha-Melitz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a>, launched in 1860.</p><p>The movement spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, where Joseph Perl opened the first modern Jewish school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> at Tarnopol in 1813 and published his satire on Hasidic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, Megaleh Tmirin, in 1819. Maskilim there worked with the Russian government of the Pale of Settlement on secular education. The pogroms of 1881 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a> ended its last stage, as many adherents turned towards proto-Zionist groups like Hibbat Zion.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/torah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torah.jpg" alt="Torah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Тора._Нацыянальная_бібліятэка_Беларусі.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Moses Mendelssohn, philosopher and man of letters, was a central founder; his students started Ha-Meassef, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> circle also included Naphtali Hirz Wessely, Isaac Satanow and Isaac Euchel, whose tracts formed the first shared literary canon. Wessely compiled the eighteen-part epic cycle Shirei Tif'eret about Moses from 1789. Aaron Halle-Wolfssohn wrote the first modern Judaeo-German play, Leichtsinn und Frömmelei, in 1796. Joseph ha-Efrati Troplowitz, the pioneering playwright, wrote Melukhat Sha'ul in 1794, and Judah Leib Ben-Ze'ev, the first modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> grammarian, published a manual of the language in 1796.</p><p>In the east, Joseph Perl pioneered satire in Megaleh Tmirin. Isaac Baer Levinsohn became known as the Russian Mendelssohn. Avraham Dov Ber Lebensohn, a leading metricist, published Shirei S'fat haQodesh in 1842. Abraham Mapu wrote Ahavat Zion, the first Hebrew full-length novel, in 1853. Judah Leib Gordon was the most eminent poet of his generation and arguably of the whole Haskalah; his best-known work was the 1876 epic Qotzo shel Yodh. Solomon Löwisohn was the first to translate Shakespeare into Hebrew. Aleksander Zederbaum edited Ha-Melitz.</p><p>Nearly all maskilim had an old-style secluded education and were young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> scholars before meeting outside knowledge. The movement was almost totally male-dominated, as women did not receive enough tutoring to master Hebrew. Most maskilim lived by private tutoring and similar work, and needed patrons, either affluent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> or state institutions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Haskalah produced the first phase of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> literature and carried out communal, educational and cultural reforms, but it was a limited part of the modernisation of Central and Eastern European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. It was weak and dependent on far stronger elements. The government wanted only productive, loyal subjects with rudimentary secular education, and had no interest in the maskilim's visions of renaissance. Rich Jews were mostly practical, hoping for a betterment that would bring emancipation and equal rights. Frustration with apathetic Jews, whether conservative fanatics or parvenu assimilationists, is a common theme in the movement's literature.</p><p>It was marked by a dual agenda. The maskilim urged Jews to acculturate to the modern state and also to remain a distinct group with its own culture. They drew a line between themselves and the pseudo-maskilim who sought full assimilation, and some were not fully agreeable to civic emancipation. The movement was largely unigenerational: the children of maskilim almost never followed their parents, and often lacked Hebrew. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> acculturation far exceeded the movement's plans, and the journal Sulamith, begun in 1806, was written almost entirely in German.</p><p>Later research narrowed the movement's importance. Historians such as Heinrich Graetz had depicted it as a major factor in the changes of Jewish society, but the contemporary historical consensus portrays it as much humbler. State schools and new economic opportunities rivalled or overshadowed it, and in many regions it had no effect at all.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodor-herzl"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theodor-herzl.jpg" alt="Theodor Herzl" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/theodor-herzl">Theodor Herzl</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Theodor_Herzl_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carl Pietzner, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Haskalah led to a secular Jewish culture with an emphasis on Jewish history and identity rather than religion, and to political engagement over emancipation, new Jewish political movements and later, in the face of continued persecutions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zionism">Zionism</a>. One commentator described emancipation as producing two opposed movements: cultural assimilation, begun by Moses Mendelssohn, and Zionism, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-herzl">Theodor Herzl</a> in 1896.</p><p>It also shaped religious life. The Reform movement was born with founders such as Israel Jacobson and Leopold Zunz, who rejected the observance of aspects of Jewish law they classed as ritual rather than moral. Groups such as the Society of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Friends</a> of Reform and the Association for the Reform of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> wanted change, for example regarding circumcision. Within Orthodoxy the Mussar Movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> im Derech Eretz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> appeared in response. Orthodox <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> opposed the reformers, who in their view insulted God by changing tradition.</p><p>Later movements judged the Haskalah harshly. The Jewish national movements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, founded by disillusioned maskilim, regarded it as a naive, liberal and assimilationist ideology that promised false hopes of equality; nationalist thinkers and historians such as Peretz Smolenskin, Ahad Ha'am and Simon Dubnow held this view, once common in Israeli historiography. Mendele Mocher Sforim's Beseter ra'am of 1886 is taken to mark the end of the Maskilic period in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> literature and the beginning of the Era of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Haskalah, or Jewish Enlightenment, was a movement among Central and Eastern European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from the 1770s to the end of the 19th century. Its maskilim revived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> as a secular literary language and urged education and integration, clashing with rabbinic traditionalists and with assimilationists. Later historians judge its effect limited.</em></p>

